   "lang": "python",
   "code": "class Solution:\n    def canJump(self, nums: List[int]) -> bool:\n        # Set goal to the last index of the array.\n        # This is the position we are trying to reach.\n        goal = len(nums) - 1\n\n        # Loop backward through the array.\n        # Start from the second-to-last index because the last index is already the goal.\n        # Stop at index 0.\n        for i in range(len(nums) - 2, -1, -1):\n\n            # Check if the current index can jump to the current goal.\n            # i is the current position.\n            # nums[i] is the maximum jump length from this position.\n            # i + nums[i] is the farthest index we can reach from here.\n            if i + nums[i] >= goal:\n\n                # If index i can reach the goal, then i becomes the new goal.\n                # This means now we only need to figure out if earlier indexes can reach i.\n                goal = i\n        # After checking every index, return True only if the goal moved all the way to index 0.\n        # If goal == 0, the start position can eventually reach the end.\n        # If not, then we cannot reach the end from the start.\n        return goal == 0",
