   "difficulty": "Medium",
   "pattern": "Linked List",
   "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/rotate-list/",
   "lang": "python",
   "code": "# Definition for singly-linked list.\n# class ListNode:\n# def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\n# self.val = val\n# self.next = next\nclass Solution:\n    def rotateRight(self, head: Optional[ListNode], k: int) -> Optional[ListNode]:\n        # Get entire length of the list\n        last_node = head\n        length = 1\n\n        # Find last position (length)\n        while last_node.next != None:\n        last_node = last_node.next\n        length += 1\n\n        # Math to reduce k to the length\n        k = k % length\n\n        # if it is a multiple of the length of the list, nothing happens\n        if k == 0:\n        return head\n\n        # Assign node at (length - k - 1)'s next value to null\n        curr = head\n        for i in range(length - k - 1):\n        curr = curr.next\n        # Save new head so it can actually print through it from the correct context\n        newHead = curr.next\n\n        # Set to null\n        curr.next = None\n\n        # Assign node at last position's next pointer to node 1\n        last_node.next = head\n\n        return newHead",
   "notes": "# Thinking\n\nWe're given the head of a linked list and we need to rotate the list by k places.\n\nWe have: \n1, 2, 3, 4, 5, k = 2\nSo we need to take two nodes from the end of the linked list (4 and 5)\nand basicaly split these nodes from the rest of the linked list\n\n[1, 2, 3] and [4, 5]\nRotate 1: [5, 1, 2, 3, 4]\nRotate 2: [4, 5, 1, 2, 3]\n\nSo that's basically it.\n\nWe take the input linked list and take the last k nodes (whatever k is) and break the linked list at that point, cut the linked list there and swap the order essentially (end of linked list to beginning of linked list)\n\nSo first, we need to get the entire length of the linked list\n- Gets us 5\n- This also gets us a pointer to the last node (tail of the linked list) and we will know length = 5\n\nNow, we want to find the position of the break (between 3 and 4)\n\nIf we are starting at 1, we want to get to node 3 so we can cut, so the number of positions we have to move from 1 to 3 is (length - k - 1)\n\nWhy?\nLength is 5, k = 2, and -1 because of indexes (we are already starting at the first node)\n5 - 2 = 3, but list[3] is 4 so we need to do -1 so it becomes list[2] which yields 3\n\nNow that we are at node 3. We will assign node 3's next pointer to NONE (null)\n\nLast thing, take the last k nodes (4 and 5 since k = 2), to point to 1, so have node 5's next pointer to node 1.",
