   "lang": "python",
   "code": "from collections import deque\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxSlidingWindow(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> List[int]:\n        # Initialize variables to store result and deque\n        res = []\n        dq = deque()\n\n        # Initialize window\n\n        # For loop for range of k\n        for i in range(k):\n\n            # While dq exists and its last element in deque is less than num[i]\n            while dq and dq[-1] < nums[i]:\n                # Pop it because it is smaller than the new element num[i]\n                dq.pop()\n            # Append new element\n            dq.append(nums[i])\n\n        # Add to result\n        res.append(dq[0])\n\n        # Rest of the list after the first window\n        for i in range(k, len(nums)):\n\n            # If the highest element in deque is equal to the abandoned element\n            if dq[0] == nums[i - k]:\n\n                # Pop highest element in the deque\n                dq.popleft()\n\n            # While dq exists and last elem smaller than new elem\n            while dq and dq[-1] < nums[i]:\n\n                # Pop the last elems/ones smaller than new elem\n                dq.pop()\n                # Add new elem\n                dq.append(nums[i])\n\n            # add to result\n            res.append(dq[0])\n\n        return res",
   "notes": "Basically this solution works because:\n\nYou start with [1  3  -1] -3  5  3  6  7\n\nInitialize the window by first iterating from 0 to k (whatever k might be):\n\n```python\nfor i in range(k):\n```\n\nThen, just add the elements to the deque\n**BUT**, we want to add the highest/max element, in the window. \nSo, it starts as nums[0] is added to the deque, then nums[1] is added and the while loop is activated.\n\nAnd, while dq isn't empty and the latest element in the deque (accesse dby dq[-1]) is less than the newest examined element that will be set into the deque:\n- pop the right element of the deque (aka the lastest element in the deque)\n- Because they are essentially useless\n- But we always add nums[i] to deque because in case it is the greatest elem in the next window resize\n\nAnyways, then we append and add dq[0] (aka the highest elem in the window size 0 to k) to the result list\n\nThen, we go iterate through the rest of the unexplored list (aka from k to len(num) - 1):\n- First in the iteration we have to check if the highest max element in the deque (aka dq[0] is equal to the element we abandoned)\n\t- If these are equal, we need to abandon it by doing dq.popleft()\n\t- This is because when the window has moved such that index nums[i - l] yields the highest number (dq[0]), it needs to remove it so it can keep the window size because otheriwse it will stay with dq[0] being the initial highest number instead of updating via window size\n\t- Then advance just like we did initially\n\t- Then append to dq\n\t- Then append to res",
